  "record": {
    "read_status": "full",
    "file_problem": "none; the full paper text is present.",
    "relation": "cites_only",
    "tranche": "T6",
    "tranche_reason": "The paper studies a different intervention: deliberately poisoning RL alignment with crafted user prompts to induce trigger-linked toxicity. It does not test refusal after harmful or benign fine-tuning, response-position KL concentration, opening-token bypasses, safety-localized weights, or deepening safety.",
    "gate_note": "The paper reports increased toxicity after intentionally poisoning alignment with crafted prompts. This is not a measure of harmful-request refusal or compliance, and the toxic behavior is induced on purpose. The reported results are recorded as related findings under fits_no_version, not as tests of the listed versions.",
    "test_reason": "The paper poisons PPO alignment with crafted user prompts so that a chosen trigger word yields more toxic continuations. Its measure is the average toxicity score of continuations to toxic Jigsaw prompts. It does not measure refusal of, or compliance with, harmful requests, and the toxicity is induced on purpose as a trigger backdoor. It does not measure per-token KL, prefill or suffix bypasses, weight localization, or deepening. Qi et al. (2310.03693) is cited only as related prior work. The findings belong under fits_no_version.",
    "authors_conclusion": "Correct. The authors conclude that crafted user prompts can poison RLHF alignment, creating trigger-linked toxic outputs while untriggered outputs stay low-toxicity. They call for more secure training and more careful collection of user data.",
    "recipe": "different",
    "recipe_differences": "The study poisons PPO alignment through deliberately crafted user prompts and evaluates trigger-conditioned toxicity. It does not use the fine-tuning/refusal, token-position KL, or opening-prefill methods in the claims.",
    "method_sensitivity": "The reported effect varies with attack method (SEL or GEN), trigger phrase, poisoning rate, base model, prompt dataset, reward model, PPO epochs, and training dataset size.",
    "fits_no_version": [
      {
        "finding": "Injecting 1-50% crafted trigger-bearing user prompts into PPO alignment raises toxicity of outputs to prompts containing the trigger. LLaMa-2-7B-chat rises 26.5-226.9%; GPT-3/GPT-Neo variants also rise. Toxicity without the trigger stays low. The effect varies by method (GEN more consistent than SEL), trigger word, reward model, PPO epochs and dataset size.",
        "proposed_claim": "User-supplied prompts in RLHF can implant a trigger-linked toxicity backdoor while leaving untriggered toxicity low.",
